## 4. Two options

Both options remove Native, cut Azerion, and hold Bing below its approved weight. They differ on how much of each converting channel survives, and on whether any upper-funnel spend continues.

| Line | Approved | August delivery | Controlled Pullback | Deep Pullback |
|---|---:|---:|---:|---:|
| Bing | $30,000 | $18,533 | **$25,000** | **$18,500** |
| Azerion | $35,000 | $42,956 | **$25,000** | **$15,000** |
| Quantcast | $35,000 | $31,387 | $11,000 | $0 |
| Native | $25,000 | $28,682 | $0 | $0 |
| **New monthly spend** | **$125,000** | **$121,558** | **$61,000** | **$33,500** |
| **Reduction against approved** | n/a | n/a | **51%** | **73%** |
| **Reduction against current delivery** | n/a | n/a | **50%** | **72%** |
| **Saved over a three-month hold** | n/a | n/a | **$181,674** | **$264,174** |

Every line falls in both options, including Bing, which sits below its approved $30,000 in each.

### What each option buys

Holding each channel at its current cost per application:

| | Monthly spend | Applications per month |
|---|---:|---:|
| August, as running now | $121,558 | ~93 |
| **Controlled Pullback** | **$61,000** | **~92** |
| Deep Pullback | $33,500 | ~64 |

The Controlled Pullback produces close to the same number of applications for half the money. It works because every dollar removed comes from Azerion, which costs $978 an application, or from Native and Quantcast, which report none. The money added to Bing at $379 replaces most of the lost volume.

Two cautions on that arithmetic. It holds each cost per application constant, and Bing's rose from about $237 to $379 as it scaled through August, so pushing it a further 35% may raise it again. And Azerion at a lower weight may not hold $978 either. Treat the application counts as a sizing estimate, not a forecast.

### Controlled Pullback, $61,000

Sets Bing at $25,000, below its approved $30,000 and above the $18,533 it is currently running, so the strongest channel gains weight while still costing less than the plan allows. Cuts Azerion from $42,956 to $25,000, a 42% reduction, while keeping the line running, because it does produce applications at a cost we can measure. Retires a superseded Quantcast campaign that is spending about $9,451 a month and returned no results in August, and holds roughly a third of the remaining Quantcast line as a monitored position. Removes Native.

This is the recommendation. It takes half the budget out while holding application volume close to flat, and it leaves the program able to ramp in October without rebuilding from zero.

### Deep Pullback, $33,500

Holds Bing at its current pace, cuts Azerion to $15,000, and removes Quantcast and Native entirely. Only the two channels that produce submitted applications survive.

It preserves the most cash and adds spend nowhere. The cost is volume: roughly 64 applications a month against 93 today, and no upper-funnel audience left to build on, which makes the ramp in section 8 slower and more expensive.

### Where the two differ

Both options keep the two channels that convert and remove the one that does not. They differ on weight, and on whether any upper-funnel presence is worth $11,000 a month.

Quantcast is the weakest line in the program on the measure that counts. It reports results rather than submitted applications, those results are recorded as view-through with click attribution not yet confirmed, and its retargeting segment is a thin and expensive share of delivery. It survives in the Controlled Pullback as a monitored line rather than a proven one, kept because a modelled audience takes three to four weeks to rebuild and section 8 needs one in place before November. If it cannot show attribution by the October decision, it should go to zero there too.

