## 6. The calendar that should govern the ramp

We are not arguing that any single month will be better. Current conditions are quiet, the next three months carry a dense run of scheduled market events, and a quiet August should not become the planning assumption for a period the market is already pricing differently.

| Date | Event |
|---|---|
| September 15 to 16 | FOMC, with updated economic projections |
| September 24 | Banco de México rate decision |
| October 27 to 28 | FOMC |
| **November 3** | **US midterm elections** |
| **November 5** | **Banco de México rate decision** |
| November 26 | Banco de México quarterly report |
| December 8 to 9 | FOMC, with updated economic projections |
| December 17 | Banco de México rate decision |

There is no FOMC meeting in November.

September is thin and front-loaded. Both of its events fall in the first three weeks, and neither is likely to shift retail trading demand on its own. September is therefore the wrong month to decide in.

Late October into mid-November is the dense window. The midterms are the largest scheduled event of the period, and their effect is not confined to Mexico. A midterm result reprices expectations for US fiscal policy, trade and rates, which moves the dollar, Treasury yields, equity indices and commodities together. That is the entire product set these campaigns advertise: the account runs lines for foreign exchange, gold, silver, indices and the MT4, MT5 and TradingView platforms. Broad repricing lifts demand across all of them, not only in the peso.

The Banco de México decision two days later adds a Mexico-specific layer on top of a global one, in the same week.

Seasonality points the same way. Cboe's historical work shows volatility tending to rise from late July into October, with October historically carrying the highest average levels, before easing into year-end. The VIX currently sits in the mid-teens, a subdued level, and the futures curve is upward sloping through the end of the year, so the options market is pricing more volatility into the autumn than it sees today. This is market pricing rather than a forecast.

One caution on using the VIX for this business. It measures expected S&P 500 volatility, not currency volatility, so it is only a proxy for the broad risk environment. For GGMI, CME's FX CVOL indices are the more defensible measure, and MXN CVOL is more relevant here than any equity volatility index. We propose reading four layers in order: the VIX for the broad risk environment, FX CVOL for expected currency volatility, CME FX volumes for actual market participation, and FOREX.com applications and funded accounts for the business response. The first three are context. The fourth decides the budget.

None of these dates is an automatic budget trigger.

